## Chapter 1.1: Crime in the Future

It took about fifteen minutes for Luka to journey on foot to the ROVD precinct. He could have waited for the scheduled bus, but he simply liked walking. Still, as much as he wanted to enjoy the city atmosphere, the -25°C Siberian winter air said otherwise. Every gust of wind that rushed against his face steadily threatened to turn him into a walking snowman.

The ROVD was housed in a sturdy, four-story neoclassical stone building constructed during the mid-twentieth century, now modernized with aerogel-insulated[footnote:fzt7s] windows, reinforced doors, and thermal wall paneling. Luka stared up at the State Emblem of the Soviet Union mounted above the entrance, asking himself one last time if he really wanted to do this. After a brief, quiet breath, he pushed the heavy door open.

%Footnote.fzt7s: An ultralight, synthetic porous solid with exceptionally low thermal conductivity, widely used in 22nd-century architecture for extreme sub-zero weatherproofing.

The warm air from inside rushed out to meet him. He couldn’t help but pause in the threshold, relaxing as his body adjusted to the sudden heat. The radiators here were definitely higher grade than the sputtering unit in his dormitory[footnote:fbl9j]. The foyer smelled of fresh floor wax, a faint drift of pipe tobacco, and ever-so-familiar coffee.

%Footnote.fbl9j: Heating is centrally distributed across the district via the municipal _Teploset_ (Теплосеть [tʲɪplɐˈsʲetʲ]: thermal steam grid). While the superheated water supply is universal, terminal radiator units vary by facility—ranging from high-efficiency modern convector units in government precincts to older, sputtering cast-iron _batarei_ (Батарея [bətɐˈrʲeɪ̯ə]) in academic dormitories.

After taking in the atmosphere, Luka surveyed his surroundings. The station seemed surprisingly quiet. This was the first time he had ever stepped foot inside a Soviet precinct; having moved here from China only a year prior for his research fellowship, his life before had been spent on another motherland. Considering the recent Time Ripple catastrophe, he had expected a chaotic precinct packed with officers scrambling on emergency dispatch. At least, that had been the reality during the initial weeks post-Ripple. Three months had passed since, but he doubted the Militsiya[footnote:fll77] had run out of crises to manage.

%Footnote.fll77: _Militsiya_ (Милиция [mʲɪˈlʲitsɨjə]): The civilian law enforcement body of the Soviet Union (operating under the Ministry of Internal Affairs / MVD), tasked with public safety, crime prevention, and municipal order.

Only two officers were visible in the lobby: one sat off to the side, nursing a morning coffee while scrolling on a mobile phone, while the other was a duty officer behind the front reception counter. Her gaze was already locked onto him, straightening her posture as she prepared for the interaction.

Since their eyes had already met, Luka walked straight to the counter.

Once he drew near, the officer gestured politely toward a visitor's chair. Up close, Luka took in more of her features. She looked young—likely around his own age—her chestnut hair gathered neatly into a regulation updo. Her crisp, dark-blue uniform featured red collar piping, unmistakably Militsiya issue. On her shoulder boards, a single red stripe bore one small golden star. Overall, she looked every bit as sharp and composed as one would expect from an officer on duty.

Luka sat down. Once their eyes were level, she spoke with calm professionalism.

“Good morning, comrade. Is there anything we can help you with today?”

Luka’s eyes darted to the side. Should he get straight to the point, or lay down the context first? Then again, what was the use in beating around the bush?

“…I’m here to report a crime. That is—well, how should I put this…” Saying it aloud felt absurd. Reporting a crime that hadn't happened yet—and might never happen—was deeply humiliating.

“It’s alright, take your time,” the officer said gently. Trained in de-escalation and citizen intake, her tone was reassuring. “I understand this might feel overwhelming, but I’m here to help. Can you start by telling me what type of crime took place? Is anyone in immediate danger?”

Luka felt the tension begin to loosen in his shoulders. He drew a steady breath and said,

“It’s an assault. Or worse... a murder. I don't really know the exact details of how it hap—”

He was cut off by the sudden look of bewilderment spreading across her face.

“Assault? Murder?” She leaned forward, alert. “I apologize for interrupting, but when and where did you witness this? We need to scramble a dispatch immediately!”

Luka realized he had walked straight into a corner. There was no active crime scene. There was no murder. Everything existed solely inside his head. If he didn't clarify that right now, he would only be wasting everyone’s time.

“I-I... um...” As ridiculous as it was about to sound, he forced the words out: “Would you believe me if I told you I saw it in a vision?”

The officer’s expression shifted gradually from urgency to skepticism.

“…A vision? Are you saying it hasn’t actually happened in real life?”

“_If my hypothesis is correct, it will definitely happen,_” Luka thought. But right now, it was nothing more than an unverified theory, and his only hope was explaining himself before she had him escorted to the hospital, repeating the same loop.

“I know how insane this sounds,” Luka said, his voice dropping into quiet sincerity. “Believe me, I know. It’s been three months since the incident, and I’ve been prescribed various anti-hallucinogens and neuro-stabilizers. None of them worked.”

She studied him for a moment before speaking.

“I’m not a medical professional, comrade, but have you taken the medication exactly as prescribed? Even then, recovery takes time.”

“Trust me, Comrade Officer, I’ve followed the regimen to the letter,” Luka insisted, fighting for whatever shred of credibility remained. “No matter what I do, the vision returns every single time I look at him…”

She looked him up and down, reading his posture and weary expression. It was obvious that this man wasn't playing a prank; he was entirely serious. She caught sight of the laminated badge pinned to his coat: a theoretical physicist from PIRM would normally be the very last person to entertain supernatural visions. He had to be genuinely desperate.

“So... you look at someone, and you see their future?” she asked.

“Y-Yes. He’s a fellow researcher. My best friend,” he replied softly.

Her curiosity got the better of her, and the question slipped out before she could catch it:

“Well... can you see _my_ future, in that case?”

Realizing that could trigger a trauma, she immediately pulled back. “—Ah, forgive me. That was unprofessional—”

“Actually, I could try,” Luka interrupted. A sudden spark of realization hit him: this was his chance to test the hypothesis and prove it to both her and himself. “I might space out for a moment, though.”

She adjusted her posture, visibly surprised that he had agreed. “Oh. Well... please proceed.”

Luka focused intently on her. The station around him began to blur, and his vision slowly melted away…
